WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.940
Dragon Spirit, the new legend, the blue dragon must rise again.

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:20.660
Dragon Spirit, the new legend.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:29.360
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:31.160
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:32.380
And I'm Joe.

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:43.340
It's called The New Legend because this isn't the same as the other Dragon Spirit games that came before it that you also didn't know existed.

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:44.380
Is that true?

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:45.820
You guys didn't know this was a franchise?

00:00:46.380 --> 00:01:00.060
Oh, I thought it was just one of those situations where the first game in an IP gives itself a subtitle to sort of like self-fulfill it becoming a franchise.

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:04.060
Yeah, there's sort of like a prologue at the beginning and I thought that was the old legend.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:11.160
Right. And I think both are valid responses and not knowing didn't matter, right?

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:16.900
It's not like, oh, should I go back to the arcade and play that one first before I can play the NES game?

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:19.320
It's not that kind of situation, right?

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:26.840
But there is a hefty plot to the game and a story behind it and everything and a reason for doing things,

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:36.680
which is good because this is a shmup and a lot of times it's just you're in a spaceship and you got to fight off an alien invasion, right?

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:46.600
This is kind of interesting that it's taking like some things you might see in an RPG and bringing them to the shmup genre story-wise, not mechanically.

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:54.860
Yeah, you're like a guy that can turn into a dragon and you then fight things as a dragon.

00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:56.740
A dragon shmup.

00:01:57.060 --> 00:01:58.840
So that's new, right?

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:01.160
It's true. It's not a helicopter.

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:03.840
It's not a spaceship.

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:05.740
It's not a guy with a gun.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:13.240
It's going to sound stupid because essentially in the NES, it's just all projectiles anyway, so it doesn't really matter.

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:14.500
But just humor me.

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:21.780
Do you prefer your shmups when they're vehicles or when they're like the weirdest things you can possibly think of?

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:29.940
It's hard to really say because shmups are kind of all over the place with whether or not I like them,

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:34.260
but I really liked this as a change of pace to vehicles.

00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:38.300
I'm not saying that I'd like, oh, it's got to be an animal or a dragon,

00:02:38.830 --> 00:02:41.920
but it's just like it's nice to see something different for sure.

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:46.960
Vehicles are like the meat and potatoes, so you always need to have that.

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:52.140
But every once in a while, you can have an animal as a little treat.

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:58.800
Yeah, it's nice that shmups aren't afraid to just have the thing you play as be whatever.

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:04.800
I would like a game in the Ace Combat style, but it's a dragon instead.

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:06.000
That would be interesting.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:09.420
You have a realistic dragon simulator.

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:12.460
Isn't that like Panzer Dragoon?

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:14.680
No, because Panzer Dragoon's on rails.

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:16.380
Oh, is it? I didn't know.

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:17.620
It's like Star Fox.

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:19.920
Well, you're right.

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:27.360
You're right. Okay, so to get right into the game, I'm not trying to like...

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:32.860
This is going to sound way too hype-y, but this is the first NES game in a while,

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:34.580
and we've been playing a lot of them.

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:36.500
We're at over 300 now.

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:44.180
The first time in a while that I was legitimately surprised by something that an NES game did

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:49.440
that hasn't been done before on the console, obviously.

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:54.840
And that is that when you start the game, you're given this prologue, as Joe mentioned,

00:03:54.840 --> 00:04:04.700
and you go right into the action, and then based on whether or not you defeat the boss at the end of this stage

00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:11.100
determines what your difficulty level is, what kind of dragon you're going to be,

00:04:11.260 --> 00:04:13.260
what happens to the story in the game.

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:21.200
It's basically like a tutorial meets a decision tree, right?

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:25.040
It's crazy, and I wonder if you guys had the same thing of like,

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:26.580
huh, what just happened?

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:28.080
Like, I've never seen that before.

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:33.600
I've never seen that before at all, even outside of the NES.

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:35.860
Like, these specific circumstances.

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:42.000
Like, the, whether or not, or however well you perform in the first level,

00:04:42.140 --> 00:04:45.480
I guess that binary is you die or you survive,

00:04:45.820 --> 00:04:49.680
it just decides the game you're going to play,

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:54.260
and you may not even know, maybe it's in the manual, I didn't read it,

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:56.580
the way that I experienced it,

00:04:56.680 --> 00:04:59.560
you may not even know that there's an alternative.

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:02.900
